  {:id     "privacy"
   :title  "Privacy"
   :blocks [[:h3 "This page"]
            [:p "This page sets no cookies, runs no script and loads nothing from other sites: its styles and its icon are inside this one file. GitHub Pages, which serves it, keeps its own logs of the requests it receives, as described in "
             [:a "https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" "GitHub's privacy statement"]
             ". The links to GitHub take you to GitHub, under its own terms."]
            [:h3 "The generator"]
            [:p "clogem-press runs on your own computer. It has no account, no telemetry and no update check, and it sends nothing about you or your content anywhere."]
            [:p "It goes online only to download a tool it needs, from that tool's own releases on GitHub:"]
            [:ul
             [[:strong "Chroma"] ", for code highlighting, on the first build of a site that has code in it, unless highlighting is off;"]
             [[:strong "Pagefind"] ", for search, on the first build of a site that turns search on;"]
             [[:strong "the fswatcher pod"] ", which watches your files, the first time you run " [:code "bb dev"] ";"]
             ["or any of them when you run " [:code "bb fetch-tool"] "."]]
            [:p "Each download is checked against a SHA-256 hash pinned in clogem-press, and kept in a cache outside your site (" [:code "~/.cache/clogem-press/tools"]
             " by default), so it happens once. A tool you installed yourself, named in "
             [:code "CLOGEM_CHROMA"] ", " [:code "CLOGEM_PAGEFIND"] " or " [:code "CLOGEM_FSWATCHER"] ", is never downloaded."]
            [:h3 "The sites you build"]
            [:p "A site built with clogem-press is a folder of files that you publish where you choose. Out of the box it makes no request to another site. It does so only when its owner turns on giscus comments or analytics (Google Analytics 4, Plausible or Umami), or gives a logo or an image as an address on another site. Search-console verification tags, if the owner adds them, are lines in the page that load nothing. A site remembers its reader's colour mode and language choice in the reader's own browser, and sends neither anywhere."]]}

